\description{
Force-directed layout of \code{.network_layered_graph()}'s underlying data
(internal, \code{network_layers.R}; built from \code{network_edges},
\code{network_enrichment}, and \code{targets_disease}): every compound, its
targets, and (opt-in, see \code{layers}) whichever pathways/diseases those
targets reach, colored by layer, node size scaled by degree, and only
the highest-degree ("hub") nodes labeled -- the same visual language as
Yildirim et al. 2007's drug-target network figure (\doi{10.1038/nbt1338}):
one big connected "hairball" plus many small disconnected components,
categorical node colors with a fixed legend, hubs called out by name.
}
\section{\code{condition = NULL} pools every built condition into one graph}{

Yildirim et al.'s clustering-by-category look is a property of the
\emph{global} drug-target network (890 drugs at once) -- a single condition's
handful of compounds cannot show that kind of structure no matter how
the layout is tuned, there just are not enough shared targets yet.
\code{condition = NULL} (default) pools every condition \code{\link[=network_build]{network_build()}} has
built into one combined graph (same compound/target/pathway/disease
node, if it appears in more than one condition, becomes one node with
edges from all of them -- not duplicated); pass one or more condition
names to restrict to those specifically, e.g. \code{condition = "FLO-ET"}
for the original per-condition view.
}

\section{Layers are opt-in beyond compound/target, on purpose}{

Default \code{layers = c("compound", "target")}, \strong{not} \code{"pathway"} or
\code{"disease"}. Two independent reasons: \code{network_enrich()} can return
hundreds of significant GO/Reactome terms, each attaching to its target
as a degree-1 leaf, which turns the plot into an unreadable dandelion
(see \code{max_pathways} for how to include pathways without that happening);
and Yildirim et al.'s own reference figure does not draw disease genes as
graph nodes at all -- they measure drug-target-to-disease-gene distance
in the interactome as a \emph{separate} analysis (exactly what
\code{\link[=network_proximity]{network_proximity()}} already does in this package), not as part of
this structural network. Both layers stay available for anyone who
wants them anyway -- just not the default.
}

\section{Labels are looked up, not raw IDs, when possible}{

Compound nodes are labeled with \code{\link[=compounds]{compounds()}}\verb{$name} (falling back to
\code{compound_id} if missing). Target nodes are labeled with their gene
symbol via \code{clusterProfiler::bitr()} + \code{org.Hs.eg.db} (same optional
\code{UNIPROT -> ...} mapping \code{\link[=network_enrich]{network_enrich()}}/\code{.network_target_pathway_edges()}
already use elsewhere in this family; falls back to the raw UniProt ID
if either package is not installed or a given ID does not map).
Pathway nodes are labeled with \code{network_enrichment$Description} when
available (falls back to the raw pathway ID). Disease nodes are labeled
with the raw EFO ID -- patliR does not currently store a resolved
disease display name anywhere, so there is nothing better to show yet.
}

\section{Derived edges are drawn lighter and dashed}{

\code{edge_kind \%in\% c("compound_pathway_derived", "compound_disease_derived")}
(see \code{.network_layered_graph()}) are transitive relationships inferred
through a target, not a directly recorded interaction -- drawn dashed
and more transparent so they read as "consequence of", not "same
evidentiary weight as", the direct edges (\code{compound_target},
\code{target_pathway}, \code{target_disease}).
}

\section{Splitting a large network into readable figures}{

A condition with ~20 compounds and several hundred predicted targets,
most of them hit by a single compound, draws as a hairball whatever the
layout. Three optional filters (all off by default) cut it down:
\code{min_target_degree = 2} keeps only shared targets; \code{compound_ids} draws
one or a few compounds' sub-network; \code{module_id} draws one module of
\code{\link[=network_module_robustness]{network_module_robustness()}}. Every filtered figure gets its own PNG
and log row (the filter is encoded in the file name and in the log's
\code{subset} column), so one call per module or per compound builds a
series of small figures -- see the examples.
}

\section{Views -- readable figures for large networks (\code{view})}{

Real conditions (~15-25 compounds, 400+ predicted targets, ~2000
edges, most targets shared by several compounds) cannot be drawn as a
readable force-directed graph. \code{view} picks one of five figures, each
answering one question:
\itemize{
\item \code{"network"} -- the historical force-directed (or \code{layout = "bipartite"}) drawing of every node; \code{layout}, \code{colour_by}, \code{layers}
and \code{max_pathways} apply. Best for small graphs or one module /
compound (\code{module_id}, \code{compound_ids}).
\item \code{"compound"} -- compound-centric summary. Compounds sit on a ring,
ordered by the similarity (Jaccard) of their target sets. Only targets
hit by at least \code{k} compounds are drawn, on concentric shells (the more
compounds share a target, the closer to the centre, the larger and the
darker it is); the other targets are collapsed into a per-compound
badge (\code{"190 targets, +9 unique"}: total targets and targets no other
compound hits). \code{k = min_shared}, or, when \code{NULL}, the smallest
\code{k >= 2} that leaves at most \code{max_targets} targets. Edge width =
prediction probability; the \code{top_hub_n} most shared targets are
labelled. \code{colour_by = "module"} colours targets by module.
\item \code{"bipartite"} -- compounds in a left column, targets in a right column,
targets grouped by \code{\link[=network_module_robustness]{network_module_robustness()}} module when available
(coloured band; compounds grouped and coloured by their own module)
and sorted by how many compounds hit them; compounds ordered by the
mean position of their targets (barycentric crossing reduction).
Edges are nearly transparent so bundles carry the message. All
targets are drawn unless \code{min_shared} is set.
\item \code{"module"} -- one shaded region (padded convex hull) per module of
\code{\link[=network_module_robustness]{network_module_robustness()}} (required), each laid out on its own
with the module's compounds pinned on an inner ring; edges within a
module in its colour, edges between modules faint grey; every
compound labelled plus the targets with the highest
\code{network_centrality} hub score in each module. Best per condition:
with pooled conditions a node takes the module of the first condition
listing it.
\item \code{"pathway"} -- compound x enriched-term dot plot: the \code{max_pathways}
most significant \code{\link[=network_enrich]{network_enrich()}} terms (restrict with
\code{pathway_db}, e.g. \code{"kegg"}), dot size = number of the compound's
targets annotated to the term, colour = share of the term's in-scope
targets the compound hits; rows and columns clustered. Needs
\code{clusterProfiler} + \code{org.Hs.eg.db}; always static.
}

\code{view = "auto"} (default) keeps the \code{"network"} figure for graphs of at
most \code{auto_max_nodes} nodes (and whenever \code{layout} is given explicitly
or \code{layers} include pathways/diseases) and switches to \code{"compound"}
above that, saying so in the subtitle. The filters (\code{condition},
\code{compound_ids}, \code{module_id}, \code{min_target_degree}) apply before every
view. In the simplified views \code{top_hub_n} is the number of target
labels (the most shared targets, spread across modules in the
\code{"bipartite"}/\code{"module"} views) and \code{colour_by} only matters for
\code{"compound"} (\code{"module"} colours the targets by module, anything else
by how many compounds share them). Colours are colour-blind safe (Okabe
& Ito, a module keeps its colour in every view; viridis for the
sequential scales); labels repel when \code{ggrepel} is installed.
}

